1
00:03:48,461 --> 00:03:54,461
It's scrumdidilyumptious!

2
00:07:36,923 --> 00:07:42,923
Willy Wonka & the Chocolate Factory is a family,
fantasy, comedy film directed by Mel Stuart.

3
00:11:25,384 --> 00:11:31,384
When eccentric candy man Willy Wonka promises a lifetime supply of sweets and a tour of his chocolate factory to
five lucky kids, penniless Charlie Bucket seeks the golden ticket that will make him a winner.

4
00:15:13,846 --> 00:15:19,846
Willy Wonka, played by Gene Wilder.

5
00:19:02,307 --> 00:19:08,307
Charlie Bucket, played by Peter Ostrum.

6
00:22:50,769 --> 00:22:56,769
Grandpa Joe, played by Jack Albertson.

7
00:26:39,230 --> 00:26:45,230
Augustus Gloop, played by Michael Bollner.

8
00:30:27,692 --> 00:30:33,692
Veruca Salt, played by Julie Dawn Cole.

9
00:34:16,153 --> 00:34:22,153
Violet Beauregarde, played by Denise Nickerson.

10
00:38:04,615 --> 00:38:10,615
Willy Wonka & the Chocolate Factory is a 1971 American musical fantasy film directed by Mel Stuart from
a screenplay by Roald Dahl, based on his 1964 novel Charlie and the Chocolate Factory.

11
00:41:53,076 --> 00:41:59,076
It stars Gene Wilder as chocolatier Willy Wonka.

12
00:45:41,538 --> 00:45:47,538
The film tells the story of a poor child named Charlie Bucket (Peter Ostrum) who, upon finding a Golden Ticket in a chocolate
bar, wins the chance to visit Willy Wonka's chocolate factory along with four other children from around the world.

13
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:36,000
Filming took place in Munich from August to November 1970.

14
00:53:18,461 --> 00:53:24,461
Dahl was credited with writing the film's screenplay; however, David
Seltzer was brought in to do an uncredited rewrite.

15
00:57:06,923 --> 00:57:12,923
Against Dahl's wishes, changes were made to the story, and other
decisions made by the director led Dahl to disown the film.

16
01:00:55,384 --> 01:01:01,384
The musical numbers were written by Leslie Bricusse and Anthony Newley while
Walter Scharf arranged and conducted the orchestral score.

17
01:04:43,846 --> 01:04:49,846
Willy Wonka & the Chocolate Factory was released in the United
States on June 30, 1971, by Paramount Pictures.

18
01:08:32,307 --> 01:08:38,307
While the film received positive reviews from critics, it was not a major financial
success, earning just $4 million by the end of its original run.

19
01:12:20,769 --> 01:12:26,769
It received a nomination for Best Original Score at the 44th Academy Awards and Wilder was nominated for
Best Performance in a Motion Picture – Comedy or Musical at the 29th Golden Globe Awards.

20
01:16:09,230 --> 01:16:15,230
The film also introduced the song "The Candy Man", which
went on to be recorded by Sammy Davis Jr.

21
01:19:57,692 --> 01:20:03,692
and become a popular hit.

22
01:23:46,153 --> 01:23:52,153
Willy Wonka & the Chocolate Factory has since become highly popular
through television airings and home video sales.

23
01:27:34,615 --> 01:27:40,615
In 2014, the film was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress, as being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

24
01:31:23,076 --> 01:31:29,076
The film explores themes of factory worker, factory,
based on novel or book, chocolate, candy.

25
01:35:11,538 --> 01:35:17,538
The tone is lighthearted.
